---
// Lesson 7.1a — Tilemaps: The Level Editor
// Grid, Tile Palette, painting a real cave.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>7.1a — Tilemaps: The Level Editor</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Dragging platform sprites one by one is a hobby; painting levels
    on a grid is an editor. Today you set up the Tilemap — the tool
    that makes a cave the size of a cave buildable in an hour — and
    learn why every level you love was drawn, not placed.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the grid.</strong> In the Platformer scene:
    <code>GameObject → 2D Object → Tilemap → Rectangular</code>. You
    get a Grid parent and a Tilemap child. Everything snaps to the
    Grid's cell size (default 1×1 — and your sprites are scaled to
    match, which is why the starter art fits).</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the palette.</strong>
    <code>Window → 2D → Tile Palette</code>. Create a new palette
    (save it in Assets/Tilemaps/Palettes), then drag the starter's
    tile sprites (look in Assets/Sprites for the cave/platformer
    ground and wall pieces) into the palette window. Unity slices
    each into a Tile. Now you have a paint tray.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — the tools.</strong> With the Tilemap child
    selected: Paint (drag), Rect (fill), Erase, Eye Dropper (grab the
    tile under the cursor), Fill (flood). Draw a floor the whole width
    of the camera, a wall, and a small raised ledge. If painting does
    not appear: check the Tilemap child is selected, not the Grid
    parent — the classic first-15-minutes bug.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 4 — the scale question.</strong> Your gauntlet
    from <a href="6.5a.html">6.5a</a> used loose platforms. Redraw its
    five sections on the tilemap grid. Where the geometry fights the
    grid (a gap that is 1.5 tiles), you now have a DECISION: change
    the gap or change the grid. Always change the gap — the grid is
    a contract with every other system (colliders, turrets, snap
    spawns).</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Editor bugs</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Paint a tile, press Play, unpaint it — did your edit
                survive? Why does the Scene (data file!) reload on
                Play, and what does that mean for "save before
                test" as a religion?</li>
            <li>Paint on the Grid parent's own Tilemap vs a second
                Tilemap child. Why will collision layers
                (<a href="7.2a.html">7.2a</a>) want SEPARATE
                tilemaps for ground vs. decoration?</li>
            <li>Zoom way in and paint a tile partially off-grid.
                What does the editor REFUSE to let you do — and why
                is that refusal the whole point of a grid?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Design Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        An editor changes how you think. With loose sprites you were
        placing; on a grid you are <strong>drawing</strong>. Do this
        now, on paper first, 30 seconds each, then paint the winner:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>A "teeth" jump: two platforms, one gap, spikes below —
            the smallest sentence in platformer grammar</li>
        <li>A "stair" — three ledges each one tile higher. Where does
            the player look while climbing — and where should the
            NEXT threat be visible from?</li>
        <li>A "room" — an enclosed space with exactly two exits, one
            at floor level and one you must jump UP to reach. This is
            the boss arena seed for Unit 8</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The AP exam describes a <strong>data abstraction</strong>
            as hiding implementation behind a representation. A
            tilemap IS your level stored as a grid of numbers. What
            does the paint tool hide — and what would you lose if
            you edited the numbers directly?
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Editor craft</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Build one 10-tile "language" of shapes (gap, stair,
                teeth, room, corridor) and reuse each at least twice
                in different sizes. Why do real level designers work
                in reusable phrases rather than one-off layouts?</li>
            <li>Decorate: can you paint BACKGROUND tiles on a second
                tilemap with no collider — what makes them different
                from ground (hint: that is 7.2a's whole lesson)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Create a tilemap + palette and paint/erase/rect/fill with confidence</li>
            <li>Explain grid-snap as a contract with the rest of the game</li>
            <li>Sketch then build the three core shapes: teeth, stair, room</li>
            <li>Explain "drawing, not placing" — the editor mindset shift</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/index.html">Unit 7: Level Building &amp; Ladders</a> — tilemap setup in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — editor windows cheat-sheet</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="7.2a.html">7.2a — Tile Collision, Layers &amp; Sorting</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="7.1a" title="7.1a Tilemaps: The Level Editor — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
